      "text": "Given the equation $|{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13}$, we start by understanding the definition of the magnitude of a complex number. For a complex number $a + bi$, the magnitude is given by $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$. In this problem, our complex number is $-4 + ti$, so we have:\n\n\\[\n|{-4+ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + (t)^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to $2\\sqrt{13}$. Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + (t)^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nNext, we square both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[\n(-4)^2 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nThen, we isolate $t^2$ by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ by taking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of $t$ that satisfies the given condition is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
